r status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>SAKIYAMA SHIRO</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>SATOMI KATSUJI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>KAJIWARA, JUN</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>KINOSHITA, MASAYOSHI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>OOTANI, KATSUHIRO</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>SAKIYAMA, SHIRO</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>SATOMI, KATSUJI</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A switching regulated synchronous rectifier comprises first and second switches (SW1, SW2) connected in series between power supply (Vdd) and ground (Vss), a switch controller (1) for turning on and off the switches (SW1, SW2), and a smoothing circuit (4) for smoothing output node potential (Vnd).  When a signal (Sc1) indicates that the output node potential (Vnd) is lower than a first reference potential (Vr1) used as a reference to detect the appearance of inrush current with the first switch (SW1) on, a control circuit (10) turns off the first switch (SW1).  In other words, the detection of inrush current is based on the voltage drop across the on-state resistance of the first switch (SW1), and this eliminates the need of an extra resistor dedicated to the detection of inrush current.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>Ce rectificateur synchrone, régulé, et de commutation, comprend des premier et second commutateurs (SW1, SW2) connectés en série entre une alimentation (Vdd) et la terre (Vss), un module de commande (1) de commutateurs, destiné à brancher et débrancher les commutateurs (SW1, SW2), ainsi qu'un circuit de lissage (4) du potentiel du noeud de sortie (Vnd). Lorsqu'un signal (Sc1) indique que ce potentiel (Vnd) est inférieur à un premier potentiel de référence (Vr1) utilisé en tant que référence pour détecter la survenue d'un appel de courant, et que le premier commutateur (SW1) est branché, un circuit de commande (10) débranche ce premier commutateur. En d'autres termes, la détection d'un appel de courant est basée sur la chute de tension traversant la résistance à l'état passant du premier commutateur (SW1), ce qui rend inutile l'emploi d'une résistance supplémentaire destinée à la détection d'un appel de courant.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>